== Free Threading <freethreading>

PEP 703 proposes making the @GIL optional in CPython @PEP703, allowing multiple threads to execute Python bytecode concurrently. We decided to use the free-threaded CPython 3.15t build to disable the @GIL and evaluate whether free-threading can eliminate the overhead of multiprocessing @PyFreeThreadingGuide. To retain the existing `multiprocessing` architecture, we extended the current implementation to use Python's `threading` module @Python315ThreadingDocs when the @GIL is disabled. By using `threading`, we avoid serializing operations, while shared memory eliminates the need for copy-on-write operations. Additionally, migrating to `threading` avoids process creation through forks, using in-memory threads instead. This also provides OS compatibility with Windows, rather than limiting execution to UNIX-like systems. This approach preserves full API compatibility with existing pygrametl workflows by automatically dispatching threads when the @GIL is disabled, while falling back to `multiprocessing` on #[@GIL]-enabled builds.
